    "result_text": "The full build passes. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners sharing the same `firstName` and `lastName` (case-insensitively), captured at creation time and returned on read.\n\n**Changes, each kept to its own responsibility:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it (and MapStruct maps it automatically by name).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the persisted `namesakeCount` field (`namesake_count` column), getter/setter, and a `toString` entry, mirroring the existing derived fields like `customerCode`/`householdId`.\n\n3. **Schema files** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) \u2014 Added the `namesake_count` column to the `owners` table.\n\n4. **`NamesakeCounter.java`** (new component) \u2014 A single-responsibility class that counts, within a collection of existing owners, how many match a candidate's first and last name case-insensitively. Follows the same `@Component` pattern as `HouseholdDuplicateValidator` and `CustomerCodeGenerator`.\n\n5. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 Wired in `NamesakeCounter`. I hoisted the existing `findOwnersByLastNameIgnoreCase` lookup (previously done inside `applyHouseholdPolicy`) up into `addOwner` and now pass that collection into both the household policy and the namesake count, so the count is computed against pre-create data with **no extra query** \u2014 reusing existing logic rather than duplicating a fetch.\n\nThe acceptance test `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
